  (:id "issue/378" :type :task :title "Is it awesome? A per-language scorecard before a column is called finished (#378)" :state :todo :evidence nil :origin "https://github.com/mas-bandwidth/schema/issues/378" :note "Glenn 2026-09-03: correct under attack, fast against the real competitor, native to that language's programmer, dogfooded, written down. Pass condition: a team with schema has no remaining reason to add FlatBuffers or Protobuf for anything.")
  (:id "issue/371" :type :task :title "Rewrite SPEC.md and SPEC-TABLES.md as ordinary specifications a person can read (#371)" :state :todo :evidence nil :origin "https://github.com/mas-bandwidth/schema/issues/371" :note "Glenn 2026-09-03, filed for later: scope, grammar, a wire diagram per construct, declarative semantics, the error cases, a conformance section; owner quotes, law vocabulary, backend status lines and history move out of the normative text. SPEC-TABLES.md is over 16,000 lines today.")
  (:id "issue/366" :type :task :title "3.0.0 gate: every feature in every language at its language's performance floor (#366)" :state :todo :evidence nil :origin "https://github.com/mas-bandwidth/schema/issues/366" :note "Feature parity is tracked by the three roadmaps on this page (packet-wire, fixed-tables, variable-tables). This task holds the other half of Glenn's 2026-09-03 ruling: a profiled sitting per feature per language, each at the most performant implementation the language allows.")
  (:id "issue/362" :type :task :title "Go tables write at 0.20x C++: the Go-shaped codec restructure (#362)" :state :todo :evidence nil :origin "https://github.com/mas-bandwidth/schema/issues/362" :note "Hold the payload pointer and offset in locals across the body and write them back once (measured 1.42x on the write body in isolation), in its own PR with the goldens unmoved, then re-measure on a quiet box. The bar: same speed as C++ or not significantly slower. docs/PORTING.md:220 holds go open on #362.")
  (:id "issue/358" :type :task :title "Rust tables read at 0.72x C++: a line-level or PMU profile of the flat decode body (#358)" :state :todo :evidence nil :origin "https://github.com/mas-bandwidth/schema/issues/358" :sprint-card "rust-tables-read-profile-finds-the-flat-decode-gap" :sprint-stream "schema-rust" :sprint-base "sprint/schema-rust" :tier "heavy" :note "Back in the sprint 2026-10-16 as card rust-tables-read-profile-finds-the-flat-decode-gap (stream schema-rust; Glenn: bring the rest of the schema C++, C and Rust work into the sprint). 98.7% of the time is self time inside the decode body, so sampling cannot name the gap. Compare the C++ and Rust decode bodies term by term on a quiet box: bounds checks, the report's counters, the clamp on read, per-field presence.")
  (:id "issue/281" :type :task :title "Merges run CI on the merge result: up-to-date branches or a merge queue (#281)" :state :todo :evidence nil :origin "https://github.com/mas-bandwidth/schema/issues/281" :note "main has no branch protection today (the protection endpoint answers 404), so two green PRs can still land a red branch. A repository setting for Glenn: require branches to be up to date before merging, or a merge queue; dev's serial stream merges cover only the stream half.")
