t, by these rules and no others: shared Go and IR code (ir/, internal/check/, internal/parser/, compiler/, internal/codegen/gotable/, tools/, .github/) takes GO TIP's version; port-specific code keeps the port's version, adapted to the new IR; spec text (docs/*.md) merges both rewrites, each statement true of the me [...]" :brief-file "docs/roadmap-briefs/schema-cards-out-2026-10-19.sexp")
  (:id "sprint-out/dart-blob-named-twice-is-blob-shared" :type :task :title "Dart: one blob record named from two slots is blob_shared: writers refuse it, the tolerant read keeps one node and counts it, the strict read refuses it" :state :todo :evidence nil :tier "pro" :needs "none" :note "Found landing sprint/schema-dart onto dev (land/schema-dart, 2026-10-21): make cards-dart-wire-fuzz and cards-dart-retain-fuzz diverge from the Go oracle, which refuses to encode a value whose two *string slots name one blob (docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §2.5, tablewire 53c68273f). The Dart wire writer numbers blobs BY CONTENT (WireState.noteBlob/blobIndex), so two equal payloads are written as one shared record, and the reader neither counts blob_shared nor marks the value so a later Save refuses. A Dart String has no pointer identity, so the port numbers a blob per slot, keeps the one decoded object on a shared read, and refuses Save of a slot still holding it; the cook packs the graph as it finds it (§7.6)." :task "Dart honours SPEC-TABLES §2.5 blob_shared on every writer and both reads; cards-dart-wire-fuzz and cards-dart-retain-fuzz green.")
